    "slug": "microsoft-office-ltsc-2024-vs-microsoft-365",
    "newTitle": "Office 2024 vs Microsoft 365: Which Is Better for Business?",
    "newExcerpt": "Office LTSC 2024, Home & Business 2024 or Microsoft 365? Compare licensing, cost over time and the right choice for Indian businesses.",
    "appendBody": "## The three Office products side by side\n\nBuyers often meet three different things under the name \"Office\" and treat them as variants of one product. They are not.\n\n| | Office LTSC Professional Plus 2024 | Office Home & Business 2024 | Microsoft 365 Business Standard |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Price excluding GST | {{price:microsoft-office-ltsc-professional-plus-2024}} one time, per device | {{price:microsoft-office-home-and-business-2024}} one time, per device | {{price:microsoft-365-business-standard}} per user per year |\n| Licensed to | A device, under volume licensing | A single PC or Mac | A named user |\n| Applications | Word, Excel, PowerPoint, Outlook, Publisher, Access | Word, Excel, PowerPoint, Outlook | Word, Excel, PowerPoint, Outlook, plus web and mobile versions |\n| Business email, Teams, 1 TB OneDrive | No | No | Yes |\n| Feature updates | None | None | Continuous |\n| Volume activation (KMS or MAK) | Yes | No | Not applicable |\n\nEach column answers a different question: licensing fixed machines in bulk, giving a few people desktop Office without a subscription, or running mail, files, meetings and applications for a team.\n\n## Home & Business 2024: the middle option people overlook\n\nIf the choice is between LTSC and Microsoft 365, the third product is easy to forget. Office Home & Business 2024 is a one-time purchase for one PC or Mac, with Outlook included and no renewal. It does not support volume activation and carries no email hosting, OneDrive or Teams.\n\nIt fits a very small business, say a three-person consultancy, where each person already has a hosting arrangement for mail and nobody needs shared storage. It does not fit anyone who wants central assignment and control of licences, because it is bought machine by machine.\n\nThe cost trap is the same as with LTSC. If you also need business email on your own domain, the subscription route usually works out better once you add the mail hosting you would otherwise buy separately. Price the whole bundle of needs, not only the applications.\n\n## A fair way to compare cost\n\nDo not compare one number against another. Build the comparison for your own estate in five steps.\n\n1. **List the devices and the people.** A perpetual licence follows the device, a subscription follows the person. Fifty laptops used by fifty people look similar either way, but fifty people with seventy devices do not.\n2. **List what you would buy anyway.** Mail hosting, file storage, meeting software, a backup of shared files, and mobile access. Put each on the perpetual side if the subscription includes it.\n3. **Set a realistic horizon.** Hardware refresh cycles in Indian offices are often four to five years. Compare over that period, not over one year.\n4. **Add the replacement event.** A perpetual licence is replaced by a project at the end of its supported life. A subscription is replaced by nothing, because it updates. Count the labour of the project.\n5. **Check the accounting treatment.** A perpetual licence is a capital item and a subscription is an operating expense. Which suits you depends on your finance team and tax position, so ask your accountant rather than assuming. For the input credit side, see [GST input credit on software purchases](/blog/gst-input-credit-on-software-purchases).\n\nSee [perpetual versus subscription licensing](/blog/perpetual-versus-subscription-software-licensing) for the principles.\n\n## Which Microsoft 365 plan, if you do choose the subscription\n\nMoving off perpetual Office does not mean buying one plan for everybody. Three plans cover most Indian offices.\n\n- **Business Basic** at {{price:microsoft-365-business-basic}} per user per year: web and mobile apps only, with email and Teams. Right for staff who do not need installed Office.\n- **Business Standard** at {{price:microsoft-365-business-standard}}: adds installed applications. The usual replacement for a desk-based perpetual Office user.\n- **Business Premium** at {{price:microsoft-365-business-premium}}: adds device management and security tooling for people who handle sensitive data or use personal phones for work.\n\nThe Business plans are capped at 300 seats in total, so firms growing past that need an Enterprise plan. Our [plan comparison](/blog/microsoft-365-business-basic-vs-standard-vs-premium) sets out the choice, and the [Standard versus Premium note](/blog/microsoft-365-business-standard-vs-business-premium) explains what the security layer adds.\n\n## Questions to ask before you sign either way\n\nFor a perpetual licence:\n\n- What is the supported end date of this exact edition, and what is our plan when it arrives?\n- Which devices will it be installed on, and can the licence be moved if a machine is retired?\n- Is the machine genuinely offline or fixed, or is that a habit we have never examined?\n\nFor a subscription:\n\n- Who owns the list of assigned users, and who removes people who leave?\n- Do we have seats that are assigned but never used? Our note on the [hidden cost of unused licences](/blog/hidden-cost-of-unused-software-licences) shows how common that is.\n- Will we cross 300 users within two or three years?\n\n## A decision rule you can write on one page\n\nChoose LTSC when a device must stay frozen, offline or validated. Choose Home & Business 2024 when one or two people need the classic apps and nothing else. Choose Microsoft 365 for every person who works with mail, shared files and colleagues. Where an estate has more than one situation, license each separately and keep a register.\n\nIf you want the estate sized this way, [send us the device and user split](/enquiry) and we will quote it as a single estate. Prices on the site are tentative, exclude GST, and a written quotation fixes the final figure.",
